Geometry under data/geo/ is licensed per source, file by file.

Natural Earth 1:110m (regions.json)
  Public domain. Natural Earth is made with the participation of volunteers
  and is free of any licence restrictions in any way.
  https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/
  Files derived from Natural Earth 1:110m vector data, version 5.1.2, taken
  from the GeoJSON conversions published in the upstream repository:
  https://github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/tree/v5.1.2/geojson
  The two files are in this repository, gzipped, under vendor/natural-earth/
  110m/, with their sha256 in vendor/SHA256SUMS; the tool reads them there.
  Regeneration is reproducible with tools/build-regions.mjs.

  land-present.json was one of these until M36a and is 10 m now — see the
  paragraph below. The lane polygons of regions.json stay at 110 m: they are
  a point-in-polygon question about which lane a place is on and nothing is
  drawn from them but a wash.

  What the import changed: coordinates are Natural Earth's own, to the
  precision it publishes. Since M39a every outline is cut at 30 degrees
  west, the meridian the map is cut at (see below); nothing else is altered.
  The cut divides Greenland, which is the one landmass there, and nothing
  else: land-present.json went from 125,938 to 126,436 bytes and
  regions.json from 221,050 to 221,298.

Natural Earth 10 m (land-present.json since M36a, base/*)
  Public domain, on the same terms as the 110 m files above, from the same
  upstream repository at the same pinned tag:
  https://github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/tree/v5.1.2/geojson
  The files are in this repository, gzipped, under vendor/natural-earth/10m/,
  and the tool reads them there: a run has no network and Natural Earth is
  never downloaded by one. The sha256 below is of each file **decompressed**
  — the file as it was downloaded — which is what vendor/SHA256SUMS records
  and what --check verifies.

  ne_10m_land.geojson            10,157,965 bytes
    1ac90796408bc6ad6911d69448485d3c4dbf2190370080368a09976e1c9f7416
  ne_10m_minor_islands.geojson    1,320,514 bytes
    8c933ca7a4760256bdc46408355706e39764b0fa01c160c28888b90b4faec29f
  ne_10m_rivers_lake_centerlines.geojson   7,307,743 bytes
    bb854a900ecbd3b408df46d5e16e3e0f974ba55993f9d8b5c26e855273c0905a
  ne_10m_lakes.geojson                     5,043,554 bytes
    2d036f53dedec578001c5c30c2959ee7d4eebc1306900fa4367c49929ec8f2d9
  ne_10m_geography_regions_polys.geojson   5,583,870 bytes
    b7b26e50ea917d3696aec87f932def2bf5f890f5770e441d59c162c6f4c92a77
  ne_10m_geography_regions_elevation_points.geojson   862,686 bytes
    f98a16867867146ec4146d6d4b18c823eeedb2825947de666116cf9a4e3f43cb

  Regeneration is reproducible with
    tools/import/naturalearth.mjs --source vendor/natural-earth/10m --check
  which verifies both sha256 before it writes anything, and rewrites every
  file it owns byte for byte on a second run.

  What the import changed. land-present.json is no longer the 110 m
  coastline: it is the 10 m land, simplified with Douglas-Peucker at 0.4
  degrees — or a sixth of a ring's own extent, whichever is less, which is
  what keeps a small island a shape and not a line — and quantized to three
  decimals, so 10,787 of the source's 446,175 points remain. Rings smaller
  than 0.007 square degrees, about 85 km², are dropped: 1,471 of the source's
  6,837 polygons survive, which is what a whole world costs inside the 200 KB
  the coastline is allowed at first paint. Malta, Bahrain, Madeira, Santa
  Maria and Graciosa in the Azores, Santiago in Cape Verde, Barbados and
  Bermuda are above that floor and are on this map for the first time; Corvo,
  at about 17 km², and the Maldives' outer atolls are under it and are not.
  The 0,0 marker Natural Earth ships as `Null island` is dropped.

  base/coast/<cell>.json is the same coastline again at the near level, cut
  on a fixed 60 by 45 degree grid of twenty-four cells and written as
  **lines** — the shore, not the shape — because a polygon clipped to a cell
  is filled and stroked and the cut edge would draw a straight line across a
  continent. It is simplified at 0.015 degrees, 144,396 points of the
  481,687 the land and the minor islands hold together, and a cell with
  nothing in it is not written at all. Every cell is cut out of one
  simplified geometry, so the cells come to exactly the world's coastline and
  no two of them disagree about where the shore is.

  Both levels are cut at 30 degrees west, the meridian the map is cut at, as
  everything under data/geo/ has been since M39a. Nothing else is altered:
  no coordinate is moved, no feature is invented, and no name is read off
  those two files, which carry none.

  base/rivers, base/lakes, base/physical and base/mountains were added by
  M36b, from the four files above, each at the same two levels: one file for
  the whole world (base/<layer>-world.json) and one per non-empty cell of the
  same twenty-four-cell grid. Simplification is the same Douglas-Peucker at
  three decimals, at the tolerance each level's cap forced:

    layer      level  tolerance  features        points kept / of
    rivers     far      0.4 deg    978/1455     5,650 / 256,386
    rivers     near    0.015 deg  1455/1455    60,661 / 256,386
    lakes      far     0.15 deg    434/1355     4,694 / 162,852
    lakes      near    0.03 deg   1355/1355    24,670 / 162,852
    physical   far     0.25 deg    543/544     10,147 /  70,307
    physical   near   0.075 deg    544/544     36,091 /  70,307
    mountains  both       none     711/711        711 /     711
    cities     far        none    1726/3086      1,726 /   7,342
    cities     near       none    3086/3086      3,086 /   7,342

  The far level of the rivers and the lakes also has a size floor, because
  what decides the bytes at that level is the feature count and not the
  tolerance: a river shorter than 1.9 degrees and a lake smaller than 0.05
  square degrees are not in the world file. Both are in the cells, at their
  own detail, and nothing is dropped from a cell for being small. The floor
  is 1.9 and not 2 because the Tejo is 1.912 degrees long.

  The physical regions keep only the seventeen FEATURECLA values that are
  landforms - Range/mtn, Desert, Plateau, Plain, Pen/cape, Peninsula, Basin,
  Depression, Valley, Lowland, Delta, Isthmus, Foothills, Tundra, Wetlands,
  Gorge, Geoarea - of the 1,047 in the file; the 503 dropped are islands,
  island groups, coasts, continents, lakes and one Dragons-be-here, which
  would have drawn the coastline a third time. base/mountains is all 711
  elevation points, each with the elevation the file gives it, depressions
  included; no height is invented and none is read off anything else.

  base/cities was added by M36c, from ne_10m_populated_places.geojson, at the
  same two levels. It is the one layer that is filtered rather than
  simplified, a point having nothing along it to take off: what is kept is
  every populated place whose POP_MAX is over 100,000 - 3,085 of the file's
  7,342 - plus every populated place a place record of this atlas names,
  whatever its population, which is one more (Panaji, 65,586). The other
  4,256 are in no file. Each city carries Natural Earth's own NE_ID, NAME,
  NAME_EN where it differs, POP_MAX, WIKIDATAID and the coordinates rounded
  to three decimals, and nothing is invented: `z` is MIN_ZOOM and `zl`
  LABELRANK, each through the same table the other layers' `z` goes through.

  The far level of the cities has a floor of its own, in people rather than
  in degrees and for the same reason the rivers' and the lakes' have one:
  a city under 250,000 is not in the world file, which leaves 1,726 of the
  3,086 there. Every one of the rest is in its cell, and a city that a place
  record names is never under the floor whatever its population.

  Names are Natural Earth's own, carried through unchanged: NAME (or `name`),
  and NAME_EN (or `name_en`) only where it differs. A feature the file left
  unnamed is written without a name, never with an empty one. Every one of
  these files is cut at 30 degrees west like the rest.

CShapes 2.0 (presences/*.json)
  Licence: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/
  NOT COVERED BY data/LICENSE. The records under data/presences/, and the
  actor records that data/actors/ holds for CShapes entities, carry
  "license": "CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0" for the same reason: they are derived from
  this dataset. Nothing derived from it is merged into a CC BY-SA record,
  and the two licences are not compatible in either direction.

  Attribution: Guy Schvitz, Luc Girardin, Seraina Rüegger, Nils B. Weidmann,
  Lars-Erik Cederman and Kristian Skrede Gleditsch.

  Citation: Schvitz, G., Girardin, L., Rüegger, S., Weidmann, N. B.,
  Cederman, L.-E., & Gleditsch, K. S. (2022). Mapping the International
  System, 1886-2019: The CShapes 2.0 Dataset. Journal of Conflict
  Resolution, 66(1), 144–161. https://doi.org/10.1177/00220027211013563

  Home: https://icr.ethz.ch/data/cshapes/
  The file imported was cshapes_2_gw.topojson, version 2.0 (package
  changelog dated 2021-05-31), taken from the CRAN package's GitHub mirror
  because the ETH site is not reachable from every network:
  https://github.com/cran/cshapes — inst/extdata/cshapes_2_gw.topojson.xz,
  decompressed with `xz -dc`.
  sha256 of the decompressed file:
  9f73468bb56aae6a6b22bb5e56bf5f3e013b9ee17c641aba37db97bcb5c1c3bc
  The copy in this repository is vendor/cshapes/cshapes_2_gw.topojson.gz —
  the same bytes, gzipped instead of xz'd, because node:zlib reads gzip and
  a run has no network. The sha256 above is of the decompressed file either
  way, which is what the tool checks.
  Regeneration is reproducible with
    tools/import/cshapes.mjs --source <file> --check --geometry-only
  for the outlines alone, or without --geometry-only for the records too;
  either verifies that sha256 before it writes anything.

  What the import changed: coordinates are quantized to three decimals and
  the topology's arcs are simplified (Douglas–Peucker, 0.1° or a sixth of an
  arc's own extent, whichever is less), so the outlines are less detailed
  than the source. Since M39a every outline is also cut at 30 degrees west —
  the map projects longitudes around 150 degrees east, which makes the
  meridian half a world away both edges of the picture at once, and an
  outline lying across it would be drawn as a smear from one side to the
  other. The cut adds points at that one longitude and removes none, except
  where a vertex was already a repeat of the one before it; no CShapes
  outline actually reaches 30 degrees west, so the shards lost 1,872 bytes
  of duplicate vertices and gained nothing: 888,209 → 888,017, 1,114,091 →
  1,113,499, 774,581 → 774,197, 846,877 → 846,781 and 880,280 → 879,672 bytes
  for the five periods in order. Since M39b each shard also carries `arcs`,
  its own list of the boundaries between the territories in it — an arc of the
  source's topology that two features share, of different entities, whose
  dates overlap — each held once and named by index from the features on
  either side; the map fills a territory's outline and strokes only these,
  because the coastline it draws is Natural Earth's and CShapes' shore beside
  it was a second shore. Those lines are the source's own vertices, taken from
  the same simplified arcs the outlines are built from, and nothing is added
  to them. A boundary the simplified outline no longer runs along is dropped
  with the polygon simplification dropped, so that no line is stroked where
  there is no territory under it. The five shards went to 961,624, 1,194,268,
  845,338, 918,615 and 957,458 bytes; `data/geo/` as a whole went from
  4,858,556 to 5,234,665, which is a fifth of the 24 MB the map block records
  for it. Nothing else is altered; every date, status, owner and
  capital is the dataset's own.

palette.json
  Derived from the CShapes outlines above — it is the graph colouring of who
  borders whom — and so it carries the CShapes licence and not data/LICENSE.
  It holds no geometry and no coordinate: one small integer per actor,
  saying which of the eight hues the map draws that actor's territory in.
  Regeneration is reproducible with tools/build-palette.mjs.

Any geometry later imported from another source gets its own directory, its
own paragraph here and its own attribution in the interface. Imported
geometry is never merged into record files (see ARCHITECTURE.md).
